LID - 1672-7347(2020)01-0096-06 [pii]
LID - 10.11817/j.issn.1672-7347.2020.180625 [doi]
AB  - Langerhans cell histiocytosis (LCH) is a rare and unexplained disease that can 
      involve in any organ or system in the body and displays a variety of clinical 
      manifestations. A 31-year-old man, who had a more than 10-year smoke history, 
      initially presented dry cough, polydipsia and diuresis, with recurrent 
      spontaneous pneumothorax. Pulmonary high-resolution computed tomography showed 
      diffuse cystic and nodular lesions. Langerhans cell histiocytosis was confirmed 
      by a transbronchial cryobiopsy. The disease is involved in the lung, pituitary, 
      thyroid, liver, lymph node, and skin. Glucocorticoid or systemic chemotherapy is 
      commonly used in the treatment for this disease. BRAF gene mutation inhibitor is 
      a new direction for the treatment.
