DP  - 2018 Sep 4
TI  - Diagnosis of Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis: Differential Diagnosis.
LID - 10.3390/medsci6030073 [doi]
LID - 73
AB  - Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) is a chronic, progressive, and fibrotic 
      interstitial lung disease of unknown origin with a characteristic imaging and 
      histologic pattern called usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP). The diagnosis of 
      IPF is a complex procedure that requires the support of various specialists, who 
      must integrate clinical, radiological, and histological data. The 
      multidisciplinary team (MDT) has become the new gold standard to diagnose and 
      manage the disease, increasing the accuracy and agreement of the diagnosis 
      between different centers. It is mandatory to exclude nonspecific interstitial 
      pneumonia or other diseases that can cause the UIP pattern, particularly drugs or 
      exposure diseases, including chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis or systemic 
      autoimmune disease. The role of the MDT is also to decide who could need a biopsy 
      or to review patient diagnoses at regular intervals in those with additional 
      information or unexpected evolution. This review provides updated information to 
      achieve a proper IPF diagnosis.
