PMC - PMC12967359
OAB - Rare lung lymphoma misdiagnosed as workplace allergic lung disease: a case report 
      and global reviewHow a rare type of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma in the lungs 
      was mistaken for allergic lung disease, and what doctors can learn for better 
      diagnosis. Why was this study important? Pulmonary intravascular large B-cell 
      lymphoma (PIVLBCL) is an extremely rare subtype of diffuse large B-cell lymphoma 
      affecting tiny lung blood vessels. It mimics allergic pneumonia, causing frequent 
      misdiagnosis and delayed treatment. Recognizing this rare lymphoma is crucial for 
      improving outcomes. What did the researchers do? The team reported a 51-year-old 
      hairdresser with a 20-year history of hair dye exposure who developed progressive 
      dyspnea. CT scans initially suggested workplace allergic pneumonia. When steroids 
      failed, doctors performed transbronchial cryobiopsy (a minimally invasive 
      procedure). Researchers also reviewed 75 similar worldwide cases. What did the 
      researchers find? Lung biopsy revealed lymphoma cells in small blood vessels, 
      confirming PIVLBCL. The patient achieved complete remission with R-CHOP 
      chemotherapy at 26-month follow-up. Literature review showed fever (75%) and 
      breathing difficulties (65%) were the most common symptoms. Critically, 29% were 
      initially misdiagnosed as lung infections or allergic diseases. Ground-glass 
      opacities appeared in 55% of cases, with reduced gas exchange function being key. 
      What do the findings mean? This demonstrates the vital importance of tissue 
      biopsy when lung problems don’t respond to treatment. Transbronchial cryobiopsy 
      proved safe and effective. Doctors should suspect PIVLBCL when patients have 
      widespread lung changes with isolated gas exchange reduction, even with apparent 
      workplace exposures. Early tissue diagnosis enables successful treatment and 
      cure.
